    <string name="info_recovery_score_title">Recovery score</string>
    <string name="info_recovery_score_body">The app\'s readiness read for today on a 0–100 scale, from Garmin\'s recovery data. Higher means you are better recovered; the sentence beside it says what that suggests for today\'s training.</string>
    <string name="info_hrv_title">Heart rate variability</string>
    <string name="info_hrv_body">The variation between heartbeats measured overnight. It is compared with your own baseline range: staying inside it, or above it, generally means you have recovered well. The arrow shows the seven-day direction.</string>
    <string name="info_body_battery_title">Body Battery</string>
    <string name="info_body_battery_body">Garmin\'s energy reserve, 0 to 100. It recharges while you rest and sleep and drains with activity and stress. Wake is the level you woke up at; used is how much has drained since.</string>
    <string name="info_recovery_time_title">Recovery time</string>
    <string name="info_recovery_time_body">Garmin\'s estimate of the hours until you have recovered from your recent training. Under 24 hours counts as ready for another hard session.</string>
    <string name="info_resting_hr_title">Resting heart rate</string>
    <string name="info_resting_hr_body">The lowest sustained heart rate Garmin measures, taken while you sleep. A slow fall over weeks usually reflects growing aerobic fitness; a sudden rise can mean illness or strain.</string>
    <string name="info_stress_title">Stress</string>
    <string name="info_stress_body">Garmin\'s stress score, 0 to 100, derived from heart rate variability: 0–25 rest, 26–50 low, 51–75 medium, 76–100 high. Lower averages mean more recovery.</string>
    <string name="info_sleep_stress_title">Sleep stress</string>
    <string name="info_sleep_stress_body">The same stress score measured only while you sleep. Low values overnight are one of the clearest signs that recovery is working.</string>
    <string name="info_respiration_title">Respiration</string>
    <string name="info_respiration_body">Breaths per minute while you sleep. 12–20 is the typical resting range for adults; a rise of a point or two above your normal can be an early sign of illness.</string>
    <string name="info_spo2_title">Blood oxygen</string>
    <string name="info_spo2_body">Oxygen saturation of your blood measured overnight. Values of 95% and above are typical; repeated low readings are worth mentioning to a doctor.</string>
    <string name="info_sleep_hr_title">Sleep heart rate</string>
    <string name="info_sleep_hr_body">Your average heart rate while asleep. It has no published normal range, so it is shown without a status: compare it with your own recent days.</string>
    <string name="info_sleep_score_title">Sleep score</string>
    <string name="info_sleep_score_body">Garmin\'s score for the night, 0 to 100: excellent 90–100, good 80–89, fair 60–79, poor below 60.</string>
    <string name="info_sleep_efficiency_title">Sleep efficiency</string>
    <string name="info_sleep_efficiency_body">The share of your time in bed that you were actually asleep. Above about 85% is generally considered good.</string>
    <string name="info_sleep_debt_title">Sleep debt</string>
    <string name="info_sleep_debt_body">How far the last seven nights fell short of your sleep target, added up. A long night does not cancel a short one. Set your target in Settings.</string>
    <string name="info_architecture_title">Architecture score</string>
    <string name="info_architecture_body">The app\'s own score, not a Garmin figure. It compares your deep sleep and REM with target shares of the night (20% and 22%) and your awake time with a 5% ceiling, weighting deep 40%, REM 40% and awake 20%.</string>
    <string name="info_training_status_title">Training status</string>
    <string name="info_training_status_body">Garmin\'s label for how your recent training is working: for example maintaining or productive. It updates after workouts, and the date shows when it was last measured.</string>
    <string name="info_acute_load_title">Acute load</string>
    <string name="info_acute_load_body">Garmin\'s training load over the last seven days, weighted by how hard each session was. The shaded band is Garmin\'s optimal range for today: inside it you are building fitness without overreaching.</string>
    <string name="info_load_ratio_title">Load ratio</string>
    <string name="info_load_ratio_body">Acute load divided by chronic load (your longer-term average). Garmin treats 0.8 to 1.5 as optimal: below it you may be detraining, above it the risk of overreaching rises.</string>
    <string name="info_intensity_title">Intensity minutes</string>
    <string name="info_intensity_body">Time spent at moderate or vigorous effort this week. Vigorous minutes count double. The bar fills toward your weekly goal, which defaults to 150 and can be changed in Settings.</string>
    <string name="info_zones_title">Heart rate zones</string>
    <string name="info_zones_body">Minutes today in each heart rate zone, from warm up (1) to maximum (5). Lower zones build your aerobic base; the higher ones build speed and threshold.</string>
    <string name="info_steps_title">Steps</string>
    <string name="info_steps_body">Your steps for the last days against Garmin\'s daily step goal, which adapts to how active you are. The dashed line is the goal.</string>
    <string name="cd_show_details">Show details</string>
    <string name="info_close">Close</string>
</resources>
